<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout xml:id="properties-congruences">
  <title>Properties of congruences</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      These are properties of congruences (aka modular arithmetic). You may use these freely in proofs as long as you cite <q>properties of congruences</q>.
    </p>
    
    <p>Throughout, let <m>n</m> be a natural number and <m>a, b, c</m> and <m>d</m> be integers.</p>
  </introduction>

  <ul>
  	<li><p>If <m>a \equiv b \pmod{n}</m> and <m>c \equiv d \pmod{n}</m>, then <md>(a+c) \equiv (b+d) \pmod{n}</md>.</p>
		<p>Basically, this means that you can substitute any number with anything else that it's congruent to at any point in addition <m>\pmod{n}</m>.</p>
		<p><em>Example:</em> We know that <m>5 \equiv 1 \pmod{4}</m> and <m>10 \equiv 2 \pmod{4}</m>, so <m>5 + 10 \equiv 1 + 2 \equiv 3 \pmod{4}</m>.</p>
	</li>
	<li><p>If <m>a \equiv b \pmod{n}</m> and <m>c \equiv d \pmod{n}</m>, then <md>ac \equiv bd \pmod{n}</md>.</p>
		<p>Like above, this means that you can substitute any number with anything else that it's congruent to at any point in multiplication <m>\pmod{n}</m>.</p>
		<p><em>Example:</em> We know that <m>5 \equiv 1 \pmod{4}</m> and <m>10 \equiv 2 \pmod{4}</m>, so <m>5 \cdot 10 \equiv 1 \cdot 2 \equiv 2 \pmod{4}</m>.</p>
	</li>
	<li><p>If <m>a \equiv b \pmod{n}</m>, then for each <m>m \in \mathbb{N}</m>, <md>a^{m} \equiv b^{m} \pmod{n}</md>.</p>
		<p>This is a special case of multiplication, since exponents are repeated multiplication.</p>
		<p><em>Example:</em> We know that <m>5 \equiv 1 \pmod{4}</m>, so <m>5^{99} \equiv 1^{99} \equiv 1 \pmod{4}</m>.</p>
	</li>
  </ul>
  
</handout>
